# "Vista Cutting Project: Area of Little Round Top, Devil's Den, the Wheatfield, and Peach Orchard" (Frederick Tilberg, Assistant Research Technician, Gettysburg National Military Park; the cover reads December 28, 1939): second reading, workstream bt-web, 2026-11-15.
# The first reading took four passages (the sources of the woodland of July 1863; the west slope of Little Round Top cut a year before the battle; the Tipton photographs; the Warren map's vegetation). This reading takes the report's statements of the wooded and open ground of the battle area, the stone works, what the Warren map shows and says of itself, and the open and wooded ground the Warren map gives around Plum Run.
# The standard text of this typescript scan interleaves a few lines (the word Open of PDF p. 8 stands after the sentence); the layout text (flag ~L) reads in order, so every quote here is cut from the layout text in cleaned form. PDF p. 9 was looked at as a page image (the survey dates read from the image).
# Dating: what the report says of July 1863 is the 1939 historian's account, resting on the sources the report names (the Warren map surveyed 1868-69, and Brady and Tipton photographs); a statement that begins 'according to the Warren map' describes the map (1868-69), which the report itself takes as the basis for the woodland of July 1863 and whose legend warns of changes since the battle: such statements are carried as indirect (a marked inference), never as the 1863 profile.
@ NPS-VISTA-CUTTING
%basis the Warren Survey Map of 1868-69 and the Brady and Tipton photographs, as the report names them
%images 9

8 | woodlots | The line of July 2: the wooded ridge north from Devil's Den, then the Wheatfield and adjacent woods to the Peach Orchard | - | 1863-07-02 | stated | The report says that when Sickles moved the Third Corps forward his left rested at Devil's Den and his line ran northward along the wooded ridge to the Wheatfield, then westward through the Wheatfield and adjacent woods to the Peach Orchard and north to the Emmitsburg Road | ={{Yfhen|When}} General Sickles moved the Third Corps {{forvrard|forward}} to its advance position, his left flank rested at Devil's Den, the battle line extending northward along the wooded ridge to the Wheatfield, thence westerly through the Wheatfield and adjacent woods to the Peach Orchard, thence northerly to Emmittsburg Road. ~L
8 | woodlots | The heavily wooded ground from Devil's Den nearly to the Peach Orchard and the wooded slopes of Big Round Top, where the Confederate attack first struck | - | 1863-07-02 | stated | The report says the Confederate brigades first struck the Federal positions in the heavily wooded area extending from Devil's Den in the direction of, and nearly to, the Peach Orchard, and that Law's brigade drove through the wooded slopes of Big Round Top | =In the Confederate attack on this line, the troops of Robertson, {{Banning,|Benning,}} Anderson, Kershaw, Semmes, Barksdale and Wofford first struck the Federal Positions in the heavily wooded area extending from Devil's Den in the direction of, and nearly to, the Peach Orchard, Law's Brigade driving through the wooded slopes of Big Round Top in his attack on Little Round Top. ~L
8 | fields | Open ground of the battle area: the fields behind the Peach Orchard, the Wheatfield, the Plum Run valley and the ground south of the Trostle and George Weikert farm buildings | - | 1863-07-02 | stated | The report says the open ground of the battle area was the fields in rear of the Federal position at the Peach Orchard, the Wheatfield west of Little Round Top, the broad valley of Plum Run between Little Round Top and Devil's Den Ridge, and areas south of the Trostle and George Weikert farm buildings | =Open ground in this area of battle were the fields in rear of the Federal position at the Peach Orchard, the Wheatfield west of Little Round Top, the broad valley of Plum Run between Little Round Top and Devil's Den Ridge, and areas south of the Trostle and George Weikert farm buildings. ~L
9 | earthworks | Stone breastworks on the western slopes of Little Round Top and a stone wall from the southern base of Little Round Top to the top of Big Round Top | E-14 | 1863-07-02 (built from the loose stone of the slopes after the attack, according to the report) | stated | The report says the loose stone along the slopes of Little Round Top made it possible for the Federal troops to build stone breastworks along the western slopes and a stone wall from the southern base of Little Round Top to the top of Big Round Top as defensive positions against further Confederate attacks | =A large amount of small, loose stone along the slopes of Little Round Top made it possible for the Federal troops to construct stone breastworks along the western slopes thereof, {{end|and}} a stone wall from the southern base of Little Round Top to the top of Big Round Top, to serve as defensive positions against further Confederate attacks. ~L
9 | general | The Warren map was surveyed from October 21, 1868 to October 23, 1869 under General G. K. Warren | - | 1868-1869 (survey; after the battle) | stated | The report says the Warren map was produced from an actual survey of the battlefield area undertaken from October 21, 1868 to October 23, 1869 under Major General G. K. Warren and is the most authentic source of information for the woodland restoration | =It is felt that the Warren Map, produced from an actual survey of the Battlefield area undertaken from October 21, 1868 to October 23, 1869, under the direction of Major General G. K. Warren, is the most authentic source of information. ~L
9 | fences | The Warren map shows woodland, open fields, period fences, boulders, tree growth, farm buildings, earthworks and contours as of the survey, and in places names a fence type that stood in July 1863 | - | ~1868-1869 (Warren map; its notes name some fence types of July 1863) | stated | The report says the Warren map purports to show woodland, open fields, period fences, boulders, types of tree growth, farm building groups, earthworks and contours as of the period of survey, and that in certain instances the map refers to a type of fence, other than the one indicated, which existed at the time of the battle in July 1863 | The map purports to show woodland, open fields, period fences, boulders, types of tree growth, farm building groups, earthworks, and contours of the Battlefield area as of the period of survey. In certain instances on the map, reference is made to a certain type of fence, other than that indicated, which existed at the time of the battle in July, 1863. | #s5
10 | general | The Warren map's legend warns that fences, roads, houses and woods changed after the battle | - | ~1868-1869 (Warren map legend) | stated | The report quotes the Warren map's legend: numerous changes have occurred since the battle in the direction of fences, new roads being opened, houses erected, woods cleared, and so on, and still calls the map the most authentic record of the physical features of the period following the battle | numerous changes have occurred since the Battle, in direction of fences, new roads being opened, houses erected, woods cleared, etc | @9: While it is noted on the map legend that
11 | woodlots | West of the Devil's Den the Warren map shows smaller trees and considerable underbrush | - | ~1868-1869 (Warren map) | stated | The report says the Warren map shows, west of Devil's Den, what appears to be a growth of smaller trees and considerable underbrush, not identified in the map legend | =To the west of the Den, though not identified in the map legend, is shown what appears to be a growth of smaller trees, and considerable underbrush. ~L
12 | fields | The Warren map shows the low ground along Plum Run toward the Trostle buildings as open ground, an extension of the swamp at the northwest base of Little Round Top | - | ~1868-1869 (Warren map) | stated | The report says the Warren Survey Map shows the low ground bordering Plum Run in the direction of the Trostle farm buildings as open ground and as an extension northward of the swamp area at the northwest base of Little Round Top | =the low ground bordering Plum Run, in the direction of the Trostle farm buildings, is indicated on the Warren Survey Map as open ground, and is an extension northward of the swamp area at the northwest base of Little Round Top. ~L
12 | woodlots | The Warren map shows wooded the open area just north of the Wheatfield Road that is open today (1939) | - | ~1868-1869 (Warren map) | stated | The report says that an area just north of the Wheatfield Road, open at the time of the report, should according to the Warren map be wooded | =To the left of this open ground just north of the Wheatfield Road is an open area at the present time which, according to the Warren Map, should be wooded. ~L
13 | woodlots | The Warren map shows trees and underbrush along Plum Run from the Trostle buildings nearly to the Codori buildings | - | ~1868-1869 (Warren map) | stated | The report says that northward from the Trostle buildings the Warren map shows a growth of trees and underbrush along Plum Run extending nearly to the upper end of Plum Run just south of the Codori buildings | =Northward from the Trostle buildings, the map shows a growth of trees and underbrush along Plum Run, extending nearly to the upper extremity of Plum Run just south of the Codori buildings. ~L
13 | fields | The Warren map shows open ground in the swale from the George Weikert farm buildings south to the Wheatfield Road | - | ~1868-1869 (Warren map) | stated | The report says the Warren map indicates open ground in the swale extending southward from the George Weikert farm buildings to the Wheatfield Road | =The Warren Map indicates open ground in the swale extending southward {{fhom|from}} the George Weikert farm buildings to the {{Wheetfield|Wheatfield}} Road. ~L
